WEBVTT

NOTE PEN&PAD interview with Alan Rector, part 2 of 4 (recorded 21 Oct 2021). English subtitles from the proofread transcript (Doc read 2026-11-10). Review version. CC BY 4.0.

1
00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:06.400
Okay, so now we're back with the second
part here about user-centered design.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.640
How did you get to all
these interesting ideas

3
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.120
and how did you work and refine this? This
would be wonderful to hear more about.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Well, we were, as I said in the very
beginning, very lucky in having

5
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.840
a Department of General Practice

6
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.240
and a group of GPs who are very
interested in working with this.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.400
and a pair of very
interesting psychologists

8
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.120
from the Medical Research Council Applied
Psychology Unit in Sheffield who came up

9
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.320
to run these workshops.

10
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.080
We both did observations and one of the
things that we did was we required all

11
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:51.120
of our programmers to and we arranged
for them to observe GPs in practice.

12
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.920
All of the IT people, had to have spent
a few days actually shadowing GPs.

13
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.760
Um, and I, this is still something
I would like to see people,

14
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.520
the people who are actually
involved in developing systems do

15
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.280
a great deal of what
happens in many systems.

16
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.480
I'm quite convinced it's because the
people who actually have to make the final

17
00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.800
detailed decisions as they're programming
and coding things, don't actually

18
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.200
understand what the problem is and what
people are going to have to use it.

19
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.760
So we did that and then we sat down
and on the one hand were brainstorming,

20
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.120
we had a sketch of sort of what we wanted
and we started trying things out.

21
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.240
It was workshops about every,
however long it took us to program

22
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.520
and so they started out every six
to eight weeks sometimes more than

23
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.000
that because it was slow at the beginning
and they ended up about every four weeks.

24
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.920
If I can share the screen for a moment,
just for a picture.

25
00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.200
Can you see that? Yes.

26
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.840
This is just one picture
that we took ages ago,

27
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.080
of a sort of workshop you have

28
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.200
that sitting at, you have a doctor
and one of the programmers

29
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:20.280
sitting at a computer in each case,
or a psychologist

30
00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.440
and one of the doctors
sitting at a computer,

31
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.600
you can see what sort of ancient
kit we were using at the time.

32
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:35.680
And I'm standing around, lots of people
are standing around taking notes.

33
00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:42.760
They were quite amazing.
Three ring circuses, actually.

34
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.240
But the thing that we saw
time and time again,

35
00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.720
was that the thing our first
ideas didn't work, as I said,

36
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.040
there were, you know, most notoriously,
there were ideas for circular menus

37
00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.520
that were the pet idea of one
of our best user interface design person,

38
00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.280
and I've since seen used well for
other things. Complete disaster here.

39
00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:11.600
The whole issue, we first did traditional,
what were then traditional,

40
00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.440
radio buttons. They were a disaster.

41
00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.320
It came with the rule
of having large square buttons.

42
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.440
We could have had rounded
square buttons or oval buttons,

43
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.800
but our software made it easier,
easier to make them square.

44
00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:31.080
But the point is they were big

45
00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.800
and everything stayed up
and you had to be able to turn away.

46
00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.600
I don't have a picture of one of the
workshops that we did where we actually

47
00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.520
brought in actors to play the part
of patients and observed what happened.

48
00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:51.640
One of the things the psychologists were
very keen on and in questioning the actors

49
00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.760
afterwards is, did they feel like
the doctors were paying attention to them?

50
00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.320
Or did they feel like the doctors
were paying attention to the computer?

51
00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:01.880
And we would very much

52
00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:06.080
like the doctors not to have been
paying attention to the computer.

53
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:12.040
And this was an iterative process.
The other thing that made this possible

54
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.080
is it was rapid prototyping.

55
00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.040
We were using Smalltalk,
the speed with which things could be.

56
00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.280
You couldn't have done this if you were
programming in a traditional language.

57
00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:24.720
You might do it now with web languages

58
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.240
and the sorts of things
we have today, but then.

59
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:31.960
The other, the other possibilities
were things like C plus plus,

60
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.240
and you just, you could not have got
things done in the time we did without

61
00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.080
an environment that let
you build things very quickly.

62
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.160
So they were often ‘cardboard'. They.
They didn't always have all the,

63
00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:48.960
we would happily build things
that were pure cardboard

64
00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.000
that didn't yet have the functionality
behind them to make them work in practice.

65
00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.880
We wanted to see if the interface would
work and we always built the interface

66
00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.800
before we built the system,

67
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.440
the rule was to build the interface
before you built the guts behind.

68
00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:14.680
And out of this we got a whole series
of slogans of which the one that I,

69
00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:21.560
that we keep talking about is trying
to make the computer do the right thing,

70
00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:24.160
that it should be useful
and it should be usable.

71
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.680
And one that doesn't show up well here,

72
00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:35.760
because the gee whiz part of it was the
user enter was the data entry forms,

73
00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.440
but we did a lot on summarizing
the medical record,

74
00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.440
and doctors varied enormously
in how they wanted it brought up.

75
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.080
But the system, Clinergy,
was not as good at this.

76
00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:52.640
It was much harder to get to work,

77
00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.840
oddly enough, because it used
the graphics more heavily

78
00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.760
and in slightly different ways,
they weren't just pictures,

79
00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:06.080
they were drawn on this fly,
it was harder in that technology.

80
00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:13.840
But wanting a doctor to be able to look
at the combination of a problem list

81
00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.320
and maybe a couple
of graphs and a timeline.

82
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.480
And some doctors loved the timeline
and some doctors hated it.

83
00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:25.080
But for some doctors
it was tremendously useful.

84
00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:29.120
In some, and not all situations.

85
00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.880
The thing people wanted timelines
for were cancer patients

86
00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.080
and patients with chronic disease,
long-term chronic diseases.

87
00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:44.000
So we did a number of these things.

88
00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.680
The other slogan that we were terribly
aware of was that the best is the enemy

89
00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.080
of the good and exhaustive
is the enemy of the useful.

90
00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:58.320
And we were quite prepared.

91
00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:04.080
The boxes in which you can type
don't show up on the Clinergy demo.

92
00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.480
but you had to have a space to type

93
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:13.240
and you had to be able to select Read
codes directly because that was a thing

94
00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:17.880
that was absolutely required.
Furthermore, many many of our doctors

95
00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:22.440
knew the Read codes for the ones
they used all the time by heart.

96
00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.920
They could tell you what I-23.6
was or they could tell you what

97
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:34.360
a baby with a sniffle was or a cold
and a sore throat was,

98
00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.840
and they didn't want to have
to go through the menu.

99
00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.360
So there were things where
it was really much quicker.

100
00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:43.920
And today with voice,
you'd probably just let,

101
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:51.000
if they could just say cough and cold,

102
00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:58.360
and stand, whatever their standard meds
were, that would probably be enough.

103
00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.320
And they, we effectively had,

104
00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.600
it was clear, it wasn't fully
implemented in Clinergy,

105
00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:09.240
partially because actually the underlying
systems Clinergy was used with,

106
00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:11.560
Clinergy was used partially standalone,

107
00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.400
but mostly as a front
end to other systems.

108
00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.560
And they already had those things.
So they didn't need those from Clinergy.

109
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.560
They pretty much put
them in for themselves.

110
00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:29.120
And the other thing we realized is it had
to be tailorable, not everybody is alike.

111
00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.200
And one of the things that was part
of our collection of ontologies

112
00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.440
or concept models or whatever,

113
00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:41.800
was a model that included the different
doctors and what they were

114
00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:48.520
and characteristics that they had in terms
of their preferences and we carried that.

115
00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:53.160
Some of that was done that way, and some
of that was done very simply in terms

116
00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:58.360
of every doctor's favorite prescriptions,
their favorite dosages, etc.

117
00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.560
The drug things, all of those things.

118
00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:07.000
There's no reason that they should
have to go through the whole rigamarole

119
00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:14.080
required to prescribe 100 milligrams
of amoxicillin twice a day for a week

120
00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.080
in detail and 16 clicks.

121
00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:22.280
If that's something
that they do three times a day.

122
00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.600
And that was the other thing that people,

123
00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:32.200
people were already
doing that in GP systems.

124
00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:37.760
And we carried it on,

125
00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:40.880
if I were doing it today,
and we'll come back to that,

126
00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:47.840
That's one of the places I'd bring in AI,
modern machine learning kinds of AI.

127
00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:51.720
I don't know how far you'd take that.

128
00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:57.200
You'd have to do another complete study
to work out where that would help.

129
00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.560
So you say AI for personalization
for a specific doctor?

130
00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:06.640
It was to each doctor.
To each practice and to each doctor.

131
00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:12.280
And if a doctor wanted
something else on their forms

132
00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:20.960
and we use that. One of my examples in
the next section will be about a system

133
00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:25.280
that we did that was used
for some trials around Nottingham.

134
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.880
around Manchester, rather,
not around Nottingham,

135
00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.680
in which the details of what went

136
00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:37.960
on the form for the same condition
were different for different trials.

137
00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:45.280
And the trial, what each
trial requires was different.

138
00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:49.960
And that was the other thing that did come
out of this and which we hope to build

139
00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:56.720
on in CLEF, which was another program
that came out out of this, which,

140
00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:04.080
well, as I say, that's another
one that got used a bit,

141
00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:09.480
but then, actually, Biobank
and other things came along,

142
00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:14.200
and the flavor of the month
changed, and I retired,

143
00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:17.760
which was one of the other things.

144
00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:24.800
what I really, what I have to say about
that. But I think it's very important.

145
00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:29.280
Important to me now, because
I don't know what will work now.

146
00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.440
Medicine is different.
Technology is different.

147
00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:40.640
I hope that somebody who tries to redo
this kind of thing will look carefully.

148
00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:48.240
If there's anything that we've learned
in healthcare IT over the last 40,

149
00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:52.920
50 years, is that it's very easy
to do things that aren't helpful.

150
00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.120
And in fact, that are unhelpful.

151
00:11:55.920 --> 00:12:01.280
And you were referring to all
the screams about "clickitis"

152
00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.440
death by a thousand clicks
and all the rest of it.

153
00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.880
It really is easy to make
things worse rather than better.

154
00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:13.000
And we need to look very
carefully at what we're doing.

155
00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:19.880
And the more we look before we get too
committed to a particular route forward,

156
00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:24.000
the less time we'll waste.
And it's very, very hard

157
00:12:25.640 --> 00:12:31.840
to get people once they've
committed to a way of doing things.

158
00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.320
Developers are very,
very reluctant to throw things out.

159
00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:38.480
That was the other thing,
the user centered design project

160
00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:42.840
Because it was rapid prototyping, getting
people to throw things out was easy.

161
00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:45.440
They only spent six weeks on it.

162
00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:48.280
Maybe they'd only spent two weeks on it.

163
00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:52.840
Throwing it out wasn't painful.
If you spent two years on it,

164
00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:55.760
throwing it out is very painful.

165
00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:01.880
So low code, no code prototyping
tools would be a good thing today?

166
00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:06.280
Essential.
I think you have to do it that way.

167
00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:11.440
I don't think you'll get it
right if you don't. It just is,

168
00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:17.240
and you have to remember, the other thing
that we learn, not from PEN&PAD per se,

169
00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:19.880
but from things we try to do around it,

170
00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:24.720
is that what works in general practice
doesn't, we did some work in geriatrics,

171
00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:29.400
and we gave up. It was a great field,

172
00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:35.160
but the mechanics of it was really then,
of it, of wheeling a then very heavy

173
00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:36.600
machine around a large cart

174
00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:43.600
[dog pause in interview]

175
00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:52.480
Excuse me. The dog walker has just
returned, and the door to this room

176
00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:57.600
is obviously not latched.

177
00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:06.200
So, and what we would need
there was very, very different,

178
00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:14.560
the things that we needed.
And the same thing is true.

179
00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:18.680
We did lots of studies,
which actually did come to something.

180
00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:24.160
We were part of the Human Center
design in how you help radiologists.

181
00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:31.600
And now you can get good enough
screens that you can do things.

182
00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:34.800
The outcome of that work
was there was absolutely no

183
00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:39.720
point in trying to replace
light boxes with screens.

184
00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:42.960
You couldn't possibly afford
enough, good enough screens.

185
00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:51.480
And that radiologists were so fast
at reading films, normal films,

186
00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:57.680
they might take forever or over
someone that was subtle.

187
00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.040
But the speed with which a practiced

188
00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:06.680
radiologist can scan a head tomogram,

189
00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:14.680
which sits as a series of what,
32 slices on film,

190
00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:20.560
and tell you if it's normal or abnormal
is quite incredible, less than a minute.

191
00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:26.200
What's wrong with it?
It may take them much longer.

192
00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:29.680
If they say it's not normal,
they'll then look at it for a long time.

193
00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:32.120
But that's how you get
to normal very quickly.

194
00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.000
And there were lots of things
like that that we didn't do.

195
00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:37.280
Of course, you don't
publish what you didn't do.

196
00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:41.360
So what do you think about the idea then

197
00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:44.360
if you're going to make
health Record systems

198
00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:49.240
that should fit everything in a hospital
and general practice is that doomed? Do

199
00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:52.640
you need to split it in modules and have
completely different ways of working or?

200
00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:59.120
You certainly have to split into modules.
You have to be able to tailor it.

201
00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:03.600
And what is important in one
place isn't important in another.

202
00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:10.080
I mean, in the extreme,
at the moment, here in Sheffield,

203
00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:16.400
we have a lovely system in the cath
lab that really works very well,

204
00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:21.840
that wouldn't be remotely what's
needed in a general practice.

205
00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:26.960
And I'd like them to communicate.

206
00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:32.680
It's absurd that the information is so
difficult to get from one to the other,

207
00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:37.720
but, but they're different problems.

208
00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:45.560
Places that are managing chronic patients
is different from managing acute patients.

209
00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:49.320
What emergency room docs need
is going to be different.

210
00:16:54.280 --> 00:16:59.000
Things like dermatology needs pictures

211
00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:04.320
and just being able to get pictures back
and forth has made a huge difference.

212
00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:07.080
I've had some dermatological problems

213
00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:12.520
where just being able to email
a photo from my camera has

214
00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:17.000
meant that I didn't have to go into
the hospital clinic during lockdown.

215
00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:23.560
look at one look at it and the
dermatologist could tell you what it was

216
00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:31.120
and reassure the GP that he was right,
and i could relax.

217
00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:39.560
One other thing i think i heard you saying
was that the the doctors in in your …

218
00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:44.280
when you were using
PEN&PAD also had options …

219
00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:46.880
so individual options …

220
00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:52.560
of how to use the system which i think
is fairly unique as well.

221
00:17:53.080 --> 00:18:00.080
Well, we tried, the doctors couldn't
actually configure it themselves.

222
00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:05.280
They had to ask somebody else to do
so, but we could configure it.

223
00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:08.920
